WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.140
Operating systems provide different mechanisms for enforcing privilege separation.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:22.760
Depending on the platform and application type, teams may use sandboxing, service accounts, capabilities, entitlements, process isolation, job objects, restricted tokens, containers, filesystem permissions, brokered APIs, or mandatory access controls.

3
00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.580
These controls can enforce boundaries even when application code has defects.

4
00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.300
Platform controls should match the operation being performed.

5
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.520
A renderer might need file and network restrictions.

6
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.900
A service might need a dedicated account with only specific directories available.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.040
A worker might need a small set of capabilities instead of broad administrative rights.

8
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:51.460
Applying controls as vague hardening is less useful than mapping each control to a clear operation and risk.

9
00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:58.860
Behavior differs across Windows, Linux, macOS, mobile, embedded, and containerized environments.

10
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:07.600
Names, permission models, inheritance behavior, service identities, filesystem rules, and available sandbox features vary.

11
00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:12.340
A design that is safe on one platform may need adjustment on another.

12
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:16.040
Platform assumptions should be documented and tested.

13
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.120
Security should be enforced where the platform can help, not only by comments or convention.

14
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:26.660
If a helper should not access a directory, the operating system configuration should ideally reflect that.

15
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:33.160
If a worker does not need network access, the environment should make that limitation real where practical.

16
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.480
Platform controls work best alongside application-level validation.

17
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:46.500
The OS can limit broad categories of access, while the broker still validates requests, authorizes callers, and records safe audit information.

18
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.160
Together they provide layered defense.
